\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The index in each irreducible representation}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{Let #{A} and #{\Gamma} satisfy \ref{fdip-013P} on #{V} over
#{k}. Suppose a finite group #{G} acts linearly on #{V}, its
order is invertible in #{k}, and the action commutes with both
#{A} and #{\Gamma}. For every irreducible representation #{\rho}
over #{k}, its multiplicity in the positive part of the generalized
kernel minus its multiplicity in the negative part equals its
multiplicity in #{V_+} minus its multiplicity in #{V_-}.
On #{\ell^2(\Lambda)\otimes S\otimes F} over #{\mathbb C},
when #{A} and #{\Gamma} commute with translations, this identity
holds in each Fourier block of \ref{fdip-000S}.}

\proof{
\p{Every invariant subspace has an invariant complement. Indeed,
average a linear projection onto the subspace over its conjugates
by #{G}, dividing by #{|G|}. Each conjugate still maps into that
subspace and fixes it pointwise. The average does likewise, and
commutes with #{G} because conjugating permutes the summands.
Its kernel supplies the complement. This averaging argument of
\ref{fdip-013J} uses only the invertibility of #{|G|} in #{k}.
Induction on dimension splits any such representation into
irreducible summands.}

\p{A nonzero equivariant map between irreducible representations
is an isomorphism, since its kernel and image are invariant.
Thus equivariant endomorphisms of #{\rho} form a division algebra.
Equivariant maps from #{\rho} to one copy of itself form a
one-dimensional right space over this algebra by precomposition.
Maps to any inequivalent irreducible summand vanish. The number
of copies of #{\rho} is therefore independent of the
decomposition and is additive under direct sums.}

\p{Write #{d=\dim V}. The summands #{\ker A^d} and #{\im A^d}
in \ref{fdip-013P} are invariant under #{G}. Their positive and
negative parts are invariant too. On #{\im A^d} the operator
#{A} is a #{G}-isomorphism between these two parts. The same
multiplicity of #{\rho} is therefore subtracted from both
ambient multiplicities when passing to the generalized kernel.
Over #{\mathbb C} the grid characters in \ref{fdip-000S} give
the translation types. Their blocks are preserved under the
stated commutation hypotheses.}
}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{MonoidAlgebra.Submodule.exists_isCompl}
supplies an invariant complement when the finite group order is
nonzero in the field.}
